  "title": "Mgeni Rasmi: Your Harambee Speech",
  "category": "social",
  "difficulty": "advanced",
  "level": 10,
  "order": 1002,
  "theme": "community",
  "culturalNote": "\"Harambee!\" (let's all pull together) is Kenya's national motto and a living tradition: communities raise money together for school fees, medical bills, weddings and funerals. A respected guest — the mgeni rasmi (guest of honour) — gives a short speech, and good speeches are stitched together with methali: \"Kidole kimoja hakivunji chawa\" (one finger can't crush a louse), \"Haba na haba hujaza kibaba\" (little by little fills the measure). Today, that guest is you.",
  "startingPoint": "Your neighbour Mama Njeri is raising school fees for her daughter. The MC invites you, the mgeni rasmi, to speak.",
  "grammarFocus": [
    "idioms-proverbs",
    "relatives",
    "subjunctive",
    "conjunctions-kwamba"
  ],
  "grammar": [
    "idioms-proverbs",
